            <ArticleTitle>Intensive Care Utilization for Hematopoietic Cell Transplant Recipients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2023-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bbmt.2015.07.026</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-8791(15)00503-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Blood and marrow transplantation (BMT) is a potentially curative therapy for a number of malignant and nonmalignant diseases. Multiple variables, including age, comorbid conditions, disease, disease stage, prior therapies, degree of donor-recipient matching, type of transplantation, and dose intensity of the preparative regimen, affect both morbidity and mortality. Despite tremendous gains in supportive care, BMT remains a high-risk medical therapy. A critically ill BMT recipient may require transfer to an intensive care unit (ICU) and the specialized medical and nursing care that can be provided, such as mechanical ventilation and vasopressor support. Mortality for BMT recipients requiring care in an ICU is high. This paper will describe the experience of the Stanford Blood and Marrow Transplant Program in developing and implementing guidelines to maximize the benefit of intensive care for critically ill BMT recipients.</AbstractText>
